  "analysis": {
    "text_description": "Problem:\nProve that angle C is a right angle\nGiven: Line AB is the diameter of the circle and point C is a point on the circle in between point A and point B\nPoint O is the center point of the circle\n\n(Printed on diagram: A, B, C, O, diameter)\n\nSolution Below:\nOA = OC = OB\nTriangle AOC and Triangle BOC are both isosceles triangles so\n∠OAC = ∠OCA and\n∠OBC = ∠OCB;\n∠OCA = x = ∠OAC, ∠OCB = y = ∠OBC\n∠ACB = ∠ACO + ∠OCB = x + y\n∠OAC = ∠BAC, ∠OBC = ∠CBA\n∠ACB + ∠BAC + ∠CBA = 180°\n∠ACB + ∠OAC + ∠OBC = 180°\n(x + y) + x + y = 180°\nx + y = 90°\n∠ACB = 90° and is right angle",
    "drawing_description": "A circle with diameter AB drawn horizontally. Point O is the center on AB. Point C is on the upper arc of the circle. Chords AC and BC are drawn forming triangle ACB. Segments OA, OB, and OC are drawn; OC is a radius up to C. Tick marks indicate OA = OB (and implied radii). Angle markings: at A, angle between OA (along AB) and AC labeled x; at B, angle between OB (along BA) and BC labeled y; at C, the angle is split by OC into two angles labeled x (between CA and CO) and y (between CO and CB). Radii are labeled r on OA/OB/OC.",
    "steps": [
      "OA = OC = OB",
      "Triangle AOC and Triangle BOC are both isosceles triangles so ∠OAC = ∠OCA and ∠OBC = ∠OCB",
      "∠OCA = x = ∠OAC, ∠OCB = y = ∠OBC",
      "∠ACB = ∠ACO + ∠OCB = x + y",
      "∠OAC = ∠BAC, ∠OBC = ∠CBA",
      "∠ACB + ∠BAC + ∠CBA = 180°",
      "∠ACB + ∠OAC + ∠OBC = 180°",
      "(x + y) + x + y = 180°",
      "x + y = 90°",
      "∠ACB = 90° and is right angle"
    ],
    "total_points": 90,
    "deductions": [
      {
        "deducted_points": 10,
        "deduction_reason": "Step '∠OAC = ∠BAC, ∠OBC = ∠CBA' is not justified: ∠OAC is the angle between OA and AC, while ∠BAC is between BA and AC. Since OA and BA are opposite rays on the same line AB, ∠OAC and ∠BAC are supplementary, not equal (similarly for ∠OBC and ∠CBA). The later substitution happens to still lead to the correct result, but this equality statement is incorrect.",
        "deduction_confidence_score": 0.9,
        "deduction_step": "∠OAC = ∠BAC, ∠OBC = ∠CBA"
      }
    ],
    "confidence_score": 0.88,
    "summary": "The student correctly uses isosceles triangles AOC and BOC to set ∠OAC = ∠OCA = x and ∠OBC = ∠OCB = y, and correctly decomposes ∠ACB into x + y using the radius OC. However, they incorrectly claim ∠OAC = ∠BAC and ∠OBC = ∠CBA (these are supplementary, not equal). Despite that misstatement, the core angle-sum argument still reaches the correct conclusion that ∠ACB = 90°."
